 ### External vs manual change detection
 
-hunkwise distinguishes between:
+hunkwise only shows changes it knows Claude Code made. When an Edit/Write/NotebookEdit call starts, Claude Code's `PreToolUse` hook lets hunkwise record the file's content. When it ends, `PostToolUse` (or `PostToolUseFailure`) gives the result. The difference between the two becomes the hunks.
 
-- **External changes** (AI tools, scripts): Detected when the file content on disk differs from the open editor buffer. These trigger review mode with inline hunks.
-- **Manual edits** (user typing in VSCode): The editor buffer matches the disk content after save. These silently update the baseline — no hunks shown.
+Every other change (typing, saving, Source Control discards, stash or checkout, formatters, other tools) silently updates the baseline. If such a change touches a file that still has Claude hunks, only the Claude hunks it overlaps are dropped (you dealt with them); the rest stay, and your change itself never shows up as a hunk.
 
-This means you can freely edit files while hunkwise is enabled, and only tool-generated changes will produce hunks.
+Only one VSCode window can listen on the port. It writes each hook event to a shared folder in the temp directory, which every window follows, and another window takes over when it closes. Changes Claude makes while no VSCode window is open are not tracked.
 
 ### File rename and delete handling
 
 - **Manual rename** (via VSCode explorer/API): hunkwise migrates the baseline to the new path. No spurious deletion hunk is shown.
-- **Manual delete** (via VSCode explorer/API): hunkwise removes the baseline. No deletion hunk is shown.
-- **External delete** (tool deletes a file): Shows a deletion hunk so you can review and restore if needed.
+- **Delete**: hunkwise removes the baseline and any hunks. Claude Code has no delete tool, so deletions never produce hunks.
 
